"The whole story runs the stages' models one after another for one cloud ({#} AU in radius, turning once every {#} million years), with a disk that has the minimum mass inside the frost line and three times it beyond (with the minimum mass there, no core reaches {#} Earth masses before the gas is gone: the timescale problem of making Jupiter this way), drawn in true distances; the bodies are drawn far larger than to scale and their orbits turn slowly on the screen, not at their real rate. Follow picks the protoplanet nearest a planet's orbit when the gas is gone, the starting planetesimals it is made of, and the cloud's gas that settles in their ring (the gas settles at j²/GM; the disk's later spreading is left out, so this marks where the material landed, not every place it went). The four stages: Collapse: the infall model of Ulrich ({#}): parcels fall in the pull of the young Sun alone, keep their spin about the axis, lose their up and down motion where streams from above and below meet in the midplane, and settle on circular orbits; the cloud's own gravity and pressure, magnetic fields and the disk's later spreading are left out. Disk: Hayashi's ({#}) minimum mass solar nebula, with the temperature of a dark grain in full sunlight (a real disk is hotter in its dense middle near the Sun), and condensation temperatures from Lodders ({#}) at {#}⁻⁴ bar. Growth: oligarchic growth (Kokubo and Ida {#}, {#}): neighbors closer than {#} mutual Hill radii join once the growth time to their joint mass has passed, so bodies end between one and two times the isolation mass (whole bodies join); a core of {#} Earth masses that forms while the gas lasts becomes a giant (how much gas it gathers is not modeled); the later giant impacts, the giants' pull on their neighbors and migration are left out. Iron cores: heating by aluminum {#} and conduction in a solid ball formed all at once (values from Johansen et al. {#}); a molten cell loses any further heat, standing in for the stirring of a magma ocean.",
